\title{The Chromatic Polynomial of the Friendship Graph:\\
A Bijective Count of Emotional Assignments}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete, closed-form determination of the chromatic counting function
of the friendship (windmill) graph $F_n$ --- the graph consisting of $n$
triangles sharing a single common vertex. We prove that the number of proper
$q$-colorings of $F_n$ is
\[
P(F_n, q) = q \cdot \big((q-1)(q-2)\big)^n,
\]
via an explicit structural bijection between proper colorings and the data of a
center color together with, for each triangle, an ordered admissible pair of
outer colors. Specializing to the six \emph{basic emotions} of psychology yields
$P(F_n, 6) = 6 \cdot 20^n$, resolving the ``graph coloring with emotions''
conjecture. We deduce that the chromatic number of $F_n$ is exactly $3$ for
$n \ge 1$, that two colors never suffice while three (and, a fortiori, six)
always do, and that the counting function detects colorability. Finally, we
connect the quantitative formula to a qualitative invariant --- the
\emph{emotional chromatic number}, the least admissible palette of size at least
three --- showing it equals $3$ for every $n$, lies within the window $[3, 6]$,
and coincides with the ordinary chromatic number for nonempty networks, where the
number of minimal emotional configurations is $P(F_n, 3) = 3 \cdot 2^n$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Graph coloring is the study of assigning labels (``colors'') to the vertices of a
graph so that no two adjacent vertices receive the same label. When the labels
are interpreted as \emph{emotions} and the edges as \emph{friendships}, a proper
coloring becomes an assignment of moods to people in which no two friends ever
feel the same thing. The number of such assignments, given a palette of $q$
colors, is the value $P(G, q)$ of the \emph{chromatic polynomial} of the
underlying graph $G$.

For general graphs the chromatic polynomial is computationally intractable, but
for highly structured families it admits clean closed forms. This paper treats
one such family: the \emph{friendship graph} (equivalently the \emph{windmill} or
\emph{Dutch windmill} graph) $F_n$, formed by gluing $n$ triangles at a single
shared vertex. Its high symmetry and its ``hub-and-spokes'' structure make it an
ideal testbed for the decomposition philosophy of chromatic enumeration.

Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A closed-form evaluation $P(F_n, q) = q \cdot ((q-1)(q-2))^n$, proved by
        an explicit bijection rather than by recurrence (Section~\ref{sec:main}).
  \item The specialization to six emotions, $P(F_n, 6) = 6 \cdot 20^n$
        (Section~\ref{sec:emotions}).
  \item A complete colorability analysis: chromatic number $3$ for $n \ge 1$,
        two colors insufficient, three sufficient (Section~\ref{sec:emotions}).
  \item A bridge to the \emph{emotional chromatic number} invariant, pinning it
        at $3$ inside the six-emotion window and relating it to the ordinary
        chromatic number (Section~\ref{sec:emo}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, a \emph{graph} $G = (V, E)$ is simple (no loops, no multiple edges)
and undirected. Two vertices joined by an edge are \emph{adjacent}. We write
$x \sim y$ for adjacency.

\begin{definition}[Proper coloring]
For a positive integer $q$, a \emph{proper $q$-coloring} of $G$ is a function
$c : V \to \{1, \dots, q\}$ such that $c(x) \ne c(y)$ whenever $x \sim y$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Chromatic counting function]
The \emph{chromatic counting function} $P(G, q)$ is the number of proper
$q$-colorings of $G$. For finite $G$ this is a polynomial in $q$, the
\emph{chromatic polynomial}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Chromatic number]
The \emph{chromatic number} $\chi(G)$ is the least $q$ for which a proper
$q$-coloring exists; equivalently, the least $q$ with $P(G, q) > 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Friendship graph $F_n$]
Fix $n \ge 0$. The vertex set of $F_n$ is
\[
V_n = \{ \star \} \cup \{ (i, 0), (i, 1) : i \in \{1, \dots, n\} \},
\]
where $\star$ is the \emph{center} and, for each $i$, the pair $(i, 0), (i, 1)$
are the two \emph{outer} vertices of \emph{triangle $i$}. The adjacency is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\star \sim (i, b)$ for every triangle $i$ and every $b \in \{0, 1\}$;
  \item $(i, 0) \sim (i, 1)$ for every triangle $i$;
  \item no other pairs are adjacent.
\end{itemize}
Thus $F_n$ consists of $n$ triangles $\{\star, (i,0), (i,1)\}$ sharing the common
vertex $\star$. It has $2n + 1$ vertices and $3n$ edges. The degenerate case
$F_0$ is a single isolated vertex.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
$F_n$ is the \emph{windmill} graph $Wd(3, n)$. More generally, $Wd(m, n)$ glues
$n$ copies of the complete graph $K_m$ at a common vertex; $F_n = Wd(3, n)$ is the
triangular case.
\end{remark}

\section{Counting a single triangle}

The engine of the main theorem is the count of admissible colorings of one
triangle once the center's color is fixed.

\begin{lemma}[Fiber count]\label{lem:fiber}
Fix $q$ colors, a center color $z$, and one outer color $a \ne z$. The number of
colors $b$ with $b \ne z$ and $b \ne a$ is exactly $q - 2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The colors to be excluded are precisely $z$ and $a$, which are distinct because
$a \ne z$. Removing two distinct elements from a set of size $q$ leaves $q - 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Per-triangle count]\label{lem:pair}
Fix $q$ colors and a center color $z$. The number of ordered pairs $(a, b)$ of
colors with $a \ne z$, $b \ne z$, and $a \ne b$ is exactly $(q-1)(q-2)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Partition the admissible pairs by their first coordinate $a$, which ranges over
the $q - 1$ colors different from $z$. For each such $a$, Lemma~\ref{lem:fiber}
gives $q - 2$ choices for $b$, independent of $a$. Summing over the $q - 1$
values of $a$ yields $(q-1)(q-2)$.
\end{proof}

\section{The structural bijection and the closed form}\label{sec:main}

\begin{theorem}[Coloring bijection]\label{thm:bij}
For all $n$ and $q$, there is an explicit bijection
\[
\big\{ \text{proper } q\text{-colorings of } F_n \big\}
\;\longleftrightarrow\;
\coprod_{z \in \{1,\dots,q\}} \; \prod_{i=1}^{n}
\big\{ (a, b) : a \ne z,\; b \ne z,\; a \ne b \big\}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Define the forward map $\Phi$ by sending a proper coloring $c$ to the pair whose
first component is the center color $c(\star)$ and whose second component is the
function $i \mapsto (c(i,0), c(i,1))$. Each recorded pair is admissible:
$c(i,0) \ne c(\star)$ and $c(i,1) \ne c(\star)$ because both outer vertices are
adjacent to the center, and $c(i,0) \ne c(i,1)$ because the two outer vertices
are adjacent to each other.

Define the inverse map $\Psi$ by sending $(z, (a_i, b_i)_{i})$ to the coloring
that paints $\star$ with $z$, paints $(i, 0)$ with $a_i$, and paints $(i, 1)$
with $b_i$. This is proper: the only adjacencies in $F_n$ are center--outer and
the two outer vertices within a common triangle, and the admissibility
conditions $a_i \ne z$, $b_i \ne z$, $a_i \ne b_i$ are exactly what those
adjacencies require. There are no cross-triangle edges to check.

The maps $\Phi$ and $\Psi$ are mutually inverse, so $\Phi$ is a bijection.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Chromatic polynomial of the friendship graph]\label{thm:main}
For all $n \ge 0$ and all $q$,
\[
P(F_n, q) = q \cdot \big((q-1)(q-2)\big)^n.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:bij}, $P(F_n, q)$ equals the cardinality of the disjoint
union on the right-hand side, a sum over the $q$ choices of center color $z$. For
each fixed $z$, the product over the $n$ triangles of the per-triangle
admissible-pair sets has cardinality $((q-1)(q-2))^n$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:pair}
(the per-triangle count is independent of $z$). Summing the constant
$((q-1)(q-2))^n$ over the $q$ values of $z$ gives $q \cdot ((q-1)(q-2))^n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The bijective proof is more informative than the deletion--contraction
recurrence: it exhibits which colorings are being counted, and it generalizes
verbatim to windmills built from larger cliques (Section~\ref{sec:gen}).
\end{remark}

\section{Emotions, colorability, and the chromatic number}\label{sec:emotions}

\begin{corollary}[Six basic emotions]\label{cor:six}
With the palette of six basic emotions, $P(F_n, 6) = 6 \cdot 20^n$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Set $q = 6$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:main}: $(6-1)(6-2) = 5 \cdot 4 = 20$.
\end{proof}

This resolves the original ``graph coloring with emotions'' conjecture: the
number of ways to assign six basic emotions to the members of a friendship
network so that no two friends coincide is $6 \cdot 20^n$.

\begin{corollary}[Colorability detection]
$F_n$ admits a proper $q$-coloring if and only if $P(F_n, q) > 0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from the definition of the counting function.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Three emotions suffice]\label{cor:three}
For every $n$ and every $q \ge 3$, $P(F_n, q) > 0$; in particular
$P(F_n, 3) = 3 \cdot 2^n > 0$ and $P(F_n, 6) = 6 \cdot 20^n > 0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
For $q \ge 3$ each factor $q$, $q-1$, $q-2$ is positive, hence so is the product
in Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Two emotions never suffice]\label{cor:two}
For $n \ge 1$, $P(F_n, 2) = 0$; no proper $2$-coloring exists.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Set $q = 2$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:main}: the factor $(q-2) = 0$, so the product
vanishes for $n \ge 1$. Structurally, each triangle is a clique on three
vertices, which cannot be properly $2$-colored.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Chromatic number]\label{thm:chi}
For $n \ge 1$, $\chi(F_n) = 3$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:two}, no coloring exists with $q \le 2$, so
$\chi(F_n) \ge 3$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:three}, a proper $3$-coloring exists, so
$\chi(F_n) \le 3$. Hence $\chi(F_n) = 3$.
\end{proof}

\section{The emotional chromatic number}\label{sec:emo}

A psychologically meaningful palette should offer at least three emotions (enough
to resolve any single conflict) and, in the basic model, at most six.

\begin{definition}[Emotional chromatic number]
The \emph{emotional chromatic number} of a graph $G$ is the least integer
$k \ge 3$ such that $G$ admits a proper $k$-coloring.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Emotional number of the friendship graph]\label{thm:emo}
For every $n$, the emotional chromatic number of $F_n$ equals $3$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:three}, $F_n$ is properly $3$-colorable, so the least
admissible $k \ge 3$ is $k = 3$ itself.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Six-emotion window]
For every $n$, the emotional chromatic number of $F_n$ satisfies
$3 \le k \le 6$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:emo} the value is exactly $3$, which lies in $[3, 6]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Count at the emotional floor]
The number of proper colorings of $F_n$ using exactly the
emotional-chromatic-number palette is $P(F_n, 3) = 3 \cdot 2^n$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $3$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:emo} into Theorem~\ref{thm:main}:
$3 \cdot ((3-1)(3-2))^n = 3 \cdot 2^n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Agreement with the chromatic number]
For $n \ge 1$, the emotional chromatic number of $F_n$ equals its ordinary
chromatic number; both are $3$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:emo} gives emotional chromatic number $3$; Theorem~\ref{thm:chi}
gives $\chi(F_n) = 3$. For $n \ge 1$ they coincide. For $n = 0$ they differ: the
lone center has $\chi(F_0) = 1$, while the emotional floor keeps the emotional
chromatic number at $3$, so the nonemptiness hypothesis is genuinely needed.
\end{proof}

\section{Generalizations}\label{sec:gen}

The bijective argument of Section~\ref{sec:main} depends only on two features of
$F_n$: the triangles are pairwise vertex-disjoint apart from the shared center,
and each triangle is a clique. Both persist when triangles are replaced by larger
cliques.

\paragraph{Generalized windmill $Wd(m, n)$.} Glue $n$ copies of the complete
graph $K_m$ at a single common vertex. The same decomposition yields
\[
P\big(Wd(m, n), q\big)
= q \cdot \big((q-1)(q-2)\cdots(q-m+1)\big)^n
= q \cdot \big((q-1)^{\underline{\,m-1\,}}\big)^n,
\]
where $(q-1)^{\underline{\,m-1\,}}$ is the falling factorial. The friendship
graph is the case $m = 3$, and $\chi(Wd(m,n)) = m$ for $n \ge 1$. The same
principle applies to book, fan, and gear graphs.

\section{Applications and discussion}

Chromatic polynomials are not merely combinatorial curiosities. The value
$P(G, q)$ is, up to normalization, the zero-temperature partition function of the
$q$-state antiferromagnetic Potts model on $G$. In computer science, vertex
coloring models register allocation and scheduling; the count $P(G, q)$ measures
the number of conflict-free schedules and its positivity certifies feasibility.
In wireless communication, coloring models interference-free frequency
assignment.

The friendship graph captures the ubiquitous ``hub-and-spokes'' motif. The
closed form quantifies exactly how a single shared constraint (the hub)
multiplies through independent local constraints (the spokes).

\section{Future directions}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Generalized windmills $Wd(m, n)$}: establish the
        falling-factorial formula in full generality.
  \item \textbf{Book, fan, and gear graphs}: other single-common-vertex gluings
        with clean product forms.
  \item \textbf{Deletion--contraction cross-check}: rederive the closed form from
        the recurrence and prove the two derivations agree.
  \item \textbf{Polynomiality and roots}: show the integer roots are exactly
        $\{0, 1, 2\}$, matching the emotional floor of $3$.
  \item \textbf{Coefficient combinatorics}: connect the alternating-sign
        coefficients to the broken-circuit interpretation.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

We determined the chromatic polynomial of the friendship graph in closed form,
$P(F_n, q) = q \cdot ((q-1)(q-2))^n$, by an explicit bijection. The formula
immediately yields the six-emotion count $6 \cdot 20^n$, the chromatic number
$3$, the impossibility of two-coloring, and a clean account of the emotional
chromatic number, which sits at the floor of the six-emotion window for every
friendship network.

